positive	0	78	577
5. Findings
•	All 106 disagreements are one-directional: annotator 1 chose positive (78) or negative (28) where annotator 2 chose neutral. There is no positive-vs-negative conflict, so the disagreement is about the neutral boundary, not about sentiment direction.
•	Typical disagreement cases are lukewarm or mixed reviews, such as "adequate", "serviceable" and "great but minor flaw". The guideline should state the rule for them (here: label the dominant or concluding stance, and use neutral only when no stance is expressed).
•	Agreement is higher on subjective chunks (90.8%, n = 773) than on objective chunks (84.6%, n = 227).
•	Rows with a high llm_confidence (>= 0.9, n = 310) show 100% agreement; the other 690 rows show 84.6%. Disagreements are concentrated in low-confidence rows.
6. Limitations
•	Two annotators were used. A third would allow Fleiss kappa and majority vote, but the assignment allows two.
•	Final label distribution is imbalanced (625 positive, 302 neutral, 73 negative), so per-class agreement on negative rests on few rows.
•	Kappa is computed against the pre-adjudication labels, so it is not inflated by the adjudication step.
